mmdevapi: stop decorating absent devices' names on every enumeration

The loop that re-creates devices present in the registry but no longer on the
system seeded each from its stored DEVPKEY_Device_FriendlyName and passed it to
MMDevice_Create as a driver id.  MMDevice_Create builds the device friendly
name from that id as "Speakers (%s)" -- "Microphone (%s)" for capture -- and
writes it back, so the stored name was fed to its own generator once per
enumeration and gained a layer each time.

The growth is unbounded, persists in the registry, and is driven by
enumeration rather than by hardware changing: any process that opens the audio
stack advances it.  A bottle that nobody had deliberately stressed held an
unplugged monitor's HDMI endpoint 97 layers deep:

  "Speakers (Speakers (Speakers ( ... (DELL S2817Q) ... )))"

Two changes.  The restore loop now reads DEVPKEY_DeviceInterface_FriendlyName,
which is where MMDevice_Create stores the undecorated id it was given, rather
than the decorated name it derives from it.  And MMDevice_Create no longer
writes the property block at all for DEVICE_STATE_NOTPRESENT: a device restored
from the registry has no driver to describe it and already carries every
property that block would write, so re-deriving them is the only way they can
drift.  Its state is still updated, which is all the restore path wants.

Identity here is the GUID the caller already has; the friendly name is a
display string and was never a safe thing to round-trip through the code that
generates it.

diff --git a/dlls/mmdevapi/devenum.c b/dlls/mmdevapi/devenum.c
index 7fd518de0f6..d718a5183c7 100644
--- a/dlls/mmdevapi/devenum.c
+++ b/dlls/mmdevapi/devenum.c
@@ -664,7 +664,14 @@ static MMDevice *MMDevice_Create(const WCHAR *name, GUID *id, EDataFlow flow, DW
     {
         HKEY keyprop;
         RegSetValueExW(key, L"DeviceState", 0, REG_DWORD, (const BYTE*)&state, sizeof(DWORD));
-        if (!RegCreateKeyExW(key, L"Properties", 0, NULL, 0, KEY_WRITE|KEY_READ|KEY_WOW64_64KEY, NULL, &keyprop, NULL))
+        /* A device restored from the registry has no driver to describe it and
+         * already carries every property this block would write. Rebuilding
+         * them from whatever string was passed in is how they drift: the names
+         * are derived from the driver id, and the id of an absent device can
+         * only come from a name that was itself derived. Update its state and
+         * leave its description alone. */
+        if (state != DEVICE_STATE_NOTPRESENT
+            && !RegCreateKeyExW(key, L"Properties", 0, NULL, 0, KEY_WRITE|KEY_READ|KEY_WOW64_64KEY, NULL, &keyprop, NULL))
         {
             PROPVARIANT pv;
             WCHAR *type;
@@ -792,9 +799,12 @@ HRESULT load_devices_from_reg(void)
         if (ret != ERROR_SUCCESS)
             continue;
         if (SUCCEEDED(CLSIDFromString(guidvalue, &guid))
-            && SUCCEEDED(MMDevice_GetPropValue(&guid, curflow, (const PROPERTYKEY*)&DEVPKEY_Device_FriendlyName, &pv))
+            && SUCCEEDED(MMDevice_GetPropValue(&guid, curflow, (const PROPERTYKEY*)&DEVPKEY_DeviceInterface_FriendlyName, &pv))
             && pv.vt == VT_LPWSTR)
         {
+            /* The interface friendly name is the driver id this function was
+             * given; the device friendly name is that id decorated. Feeding the
+             * decorated one back in made every enumeration decorate it again. */
             MMDevice_Create(pv.pwszVal, &guid, curflow,
                     DEVICE_STATE_NOTPRESENT, FALSE);
             CoTaskMemFree(pv.pwszVal);
